  "query_preview": "(Wednesday night through Sunday)...\nThat low pressure system will move slowly eastward and inland into\nsouthern California for Wednesday night and Thursday. This will\nbring showers and a chance of thunderstorms to southwestern\nCalifornia, most likely for Wednesday night through Thursday\nafternoon. \n\nThe ECMWF Extreme Forecast Index has rainfall across coastal \nportions of Orange and Los Angeles Counties in the 80th to 90th \npercentile. Cold air aloft with the low pressure system passing \nover near record warm water off the coast will increase the \ninstability with the ECMWF Extreme Forecast Index having CAPE over\nthe coastal waters into coastal waters in the 80th to 90th \npercentile and locally in the 90th to 95th percentile. \n\nFor Orange and southwestern San Bernardino Counties, rainfall \ncould range from around 1 inch for the Orange County coastal plain\nto around 1.5 inches on the coastal slopes of the mountains \nwith amounts decreasing from north to south across San Diego \nCounty.\n\nHigh temperatures will be coolest on Thursday, as much as 15 to 20\ndegrees below average for the mountains. High temperatures on\nThursday will be mostly in the 60s to around 70 for the coast and\nvalleys and mostly in the 40s and 50s for the mountains.\n\nDrier and warmer weather is expected for next weekend, but with\ngrowing spread in just how much warming for Saturday through\nMonday. HeatRisk is forecast to remain below Moderate for next\nweekend.",
